<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/8th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Syro-Ephraimite War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 735–732 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Syro-Ephraimite War was a conflict of about 735–732 BCE in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rezin">Rezin</a> of Aram-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pekah">Pekah of Israel</a>, or Ephraim, attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> after its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahaz">Ahaz</a> refused to join their alliance against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>. Ahaz appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-iii">Tiglath-pileser III</a>, who took Damascus, annexed much of Israel's territory and made Judah an Assyrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. The crisis forms the background to prophecies in the Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a>.</p></div>
